      "id": "R01",
      "abstract": "Collecting manipulation data across diverse scenes requires consistent robot control across collection sites. We present DROID, a distributed manipulation dataset and reproducible collection platform used at 13 institutions. A shared portable Franka Panda system enables collectors to change scenes while retaining common hardware. Within each scene, collectors calibrate cameras and teleoperate randomly sampled feasible tasks; periodic prompts vary camera placement, lighting, clutter, or robot base position. The dataset contains 76,000 successful episodes spanning 564 scenes, accompanied by crowdsourced language instructions. We release collection instructions and policy-training resources alongside the data. Evaluation uses existing language-conditioned diffusion policies trained separately for six tasks in four locations on DROID hardware. Mixing in-domain demonstrations and successful DROID trajectories equally yields higher mean success than in-domain-only training or equal-share co-training with a curated Open X-Embodiment mixture. All three conditions use the same policy backbone. Across the six tasks, the gains over the next-best method are 22 percentage points in-distribution and 17 under task-specific out-of-distribution changes. A fixed-size subset comparison further supports scene diversity on the challenging shifted tasks studied.",
      "methods": "A portable Franka Panda platform with two adjustable external stereo cameras, a wrist stereo camera, and VR teleoperation standardizes DROID collection while allowing scene changes. Collectors position and calibrate cameras in each scene, enter feasible tasks, and receive a randomly sampled task for each episode. Periodic prompts vary camera placement, lighting, clutter, or base position to broaden coverage. To evaluate the data, separate language-conditioned diffusion policies are trained for six tasks in four locations. DROID co-training mixes in-domain demonstrations and successful DROID trajectories equally, excluding unsuccessful trajectories from training. Comparators use the same backbone with either in-domain demonstrations alone or equal-share co-training with a curated Open X-Embodiment mixture. Each method and task setting receives ten rollouts on DROID hardware, testing perturbed initial robot and object positions within the demonstration distribution and task-specific out-of-distribution changes.",
      "results": "With the diffusion-policy backbone unchanged, DROID co-training produced higher across-task mean success than in-domain-only training or co-training with a curated Open X-Embodiment mixture. Both co-training conditions mixed in-domain demonstrations and supplementary trajectories equally; only successful DROID trajectories entered training. Across six tasks in four locations on DROID hardware, DROID exceeded the next-best method by 22 percentage points in-distribution and 17 under task-specific out-of-distribution shifts. The main comparison used ten rollouts per method and task setting; reported error bars represent standard error around across-task means. The scene-diversity check used two subsets of 7,362 successful demonstrations, each mixed equally with in-domain data. Uniformly sampled trajectories spanning more scenes outperformed trajectories from the 20 most represented scenes on the challenging shifted tasks studied. This secondary comparison supports a role for scene diversity, but does not isolate it from other subset differences.",
      "notes": {
        "abstract": "D01-D09 anchor the collection design, resource release, and utility tests. The edit retains dataset and platform contributions while clarifying the shared-backbone comparison. Read-back ties the percentage-point gains to six-task averages on DROID hardware and the diversity finding to the challenging shifted tasks, without implying a new learning algorithm.",
        "methods": "D01-D03 and D05-D07 support the collection and evaluation sequence. Read-back recovers how collectors select and vary scenes, which trajectories enter co-training, and how the shared-backbone baselines compare data sources. Unsuccessful trajectories are excluded from training, not described as absent from the release.",
        "results": "D05-D08 support the main data-source comparison and fixed-size diversity check. Read-back preserves task averages, percentage-point units, and standard error around across-task means. The ten-rollout count is attached to the main comparison; the secondary finding remains scoped to challenging shifted tasks and does not isolate scene diversity causally."
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "R02",
      "abstract": "Control and visual discrepancies complicate simulated evaluation of policies trained on real robot data. We present SIMPLER, a framework for comparing relative policy performance and behavior rather than reproducing exact physical success rates. It replays actions from existing real demonstrations and tunes controller stiffness and damping to reduce mismatch between simulated and real end-effector motion. Visual Matching combines simulated robots and interactive objects with an inpainted real-scene background, then adjusts robot and object textures to better match their real counterparts. Mean Maximum Rank Violation measures ranking disagreement through the real-world success-rate margins of misordered policy pairs. Policies are evaluated on Google Robot and WidowX hardware and in corresponding SAPIEN setups, primarily on rigid-body tasks. For six policy checkpoints across three Google Robot task groups, average rank violation is 0.056 with Visual Matching, compared with 0.143 for averaging randomized visual variants and 0.375 for validation action mean squared error. Physical arm-wrapping experiments also support simulation's suggestion that Octo-Base is more arm-texture-sensitive than RT-1-X. The ranking and physical checks support informative policy comparisons within these tested robot setups.",
      "methods": "SIMPLER compares existing policies after reducing differences between simulated and real action effects and visual observations. For control alignment, system identification replays actions from existing real demonstrations, measures differences in end-effector translation and rotation, and uses simulated annealing to tune controller stiffness and damping. Visual Matching combines segmented simulated robots and interactive objects with an inpainted real-scene background, then adjusts or projects textures to better match real appearances. Variant Aggregation instead averages outcomes over randomized visual variants. Existing policies are evaluated on real Google Robot and WidowX setups and their SAPIEN counterparts, mainly for rigid-body tasks. Mean Maximum Rank Violation assigns each policy the largest real-world success-rate margin of a misordered policy pair involving that policy, then averages over policies. Lower values indicate closer ranking agreement; Pearson correlation separately measures linear association.",
      "results": "Visual Matching gave closer average agreement with real-world policy rankings than Variant Aggregation or validation action mean squared error for six checkpoints across three Google Robot task groups. Mean Maximum Rank Violation penalizes incorrect ordering by real-world success-rate margins; it averaged 0.056, 0.143, and 0.375 for these approaches, respectively, with lower values indicating closer agreement. This comparison favors directly matching appearance over averaging randomized visual variants for the tested ranking task; action prediction error was a weaker ranking signal. A complementary behavioral check linked simulated sensitivity to a physical intervention: simulation suggested that Octo-Base was more sensitive to arm texture than RT-1-X, and new real arm-wrapping experiments supported that relative sensitivity. The evidence supports policy ranking and relative robustness comparisons in the evaluated setups, while some checkpoints remained sensitive to visual differences. Agreement therefore does not establish exact reproduction of physical success rates.",
      "notes": {
        "abstract": "S01-S07 and S09 support the framework, alignment operations, ranking metric, and physical behavioral check. Read-back retains these contributions, specifies the six-checkpoint/three-task-group comparison, and connects the arm-texture finding to Octo-Base versus RT-1-X. Neither ranking agreement nor the physical check becomes an absolute-fidelity claim.",
        "methods": "S02-S06 anchor the operational sequence and evaluation design. Read-back distinguishes real hardware from SAPIEN counterparts and explains the controller and appearance adjustments. The metric now explicitly takes each policy's worst misordering margin before averaging across policies; Pearson correlation remains a separate linear-association measure.",
        "results": "S02 and S07 anchor the ranking comparison; S09 supports the physical sensitivity check, and S08 the residual visual-sensitivity qualification. Read-back distinguishes ranking agreement from action prediction accuracy and physical success rates. The arm-wrapping result supports the relative texture sensitivity of the named policies, not general simulator fidelity."
      }
